00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
so david thank you very much for being  with us this morning and good morning to  you uh so just first of all your  reaction to what we're seeing for these  next couple of days i mean we always  fear that we reach these temperatures  but have we reached them sooner than  scientists expected  uh  if if you ask me that question about  what scientists expected 10 years ago  the answer is yes but we we have now  seen a new weather pattern emerging  which is impacting the whole of the  northern hemisphere  and it will also impact the southern  hemisphere and what i've talked about  here is the fact that the arctic circle  region has been over the last 10 years  heating up about four times faster than  the rest of the planet which means that  the average temperature above the  level that existed before we started  burning fossil fuels  it is about 3.5 degrees centigrade above  that level  and for the rest of the planet we're on  1.35  and rapidly approaching 1.5 so  this is something we hadn't quite  anticipated the loss of ice over the  arctic ocean  has proceeded much more quickly than any  climate scientist predicted and today 50  percent of the arctic ocean is exposed  to sunlight and that polar summer brings  the sun right right through the day  and so what what we have is the arctic  circle region becoming one of the warmer  regions of the northern hemisphere  during the three polar summer months  and that is driving the temperatures  around the world in a very strange way  it's led by  this wind that blows  in an anti-clockwise direction around  the north pole  this this wind  is a strong wind  and it's been blowing in a virtual  circle around the north pole for a long  time  it is now severely distorted and the  result of that distortion is all of the  extreme weather events we're seeing now  the heat wave we're seeing is coming up  from  the equatorial region in africa through  north africa through spain and portugal  portugal experiencing temperatures of 47  degrees centigrade  that's a record in portugal for for july  and  and now this year hitting britain as  well i think it's critically important  to understand that in previous heat  waves over the last three years we have  managed to escape them although the  highest  previous temperature recorded in england  was of course in cambridge in 2019 where  the temperature of 38.9 degrees  centigrade was was recorded  so all of this  is now predictable  and we have to revise our opinions these  heat waves will repeat themselves and  get more severe going forward in time  we're not going to have to wait to the  end of the century for that  to happen it's already happening on a  regular basis around the northern  hemisphere david it's bad and it's going  to get worse so that leaves the question  what can we do about it at this stage  so  as emergencies all of the advice that's  being given must be taken  everyone has to understand even a fit  healthy person is at risk our normal  body temperature is 37 degrees  any temperature above 37 even in the  shade  is therefore putting us at risk you're  needing to get rid of that excess heat  the way we do that this is through  perspiration  but if we perspire  by dripping we're not perspiring by  evaporating water into the air as water  vapor  and we lose all the cooling advantage in  that process the heat required to turn  water into vapor is what is taken from  your body in that normal perspiration  process but if we start dripping sweat  then it becomes very dangerous so i  think two things one stay in a cool  place of course  two  look for a windy place as well so that  you're getting the wind to blow the  perspiration off your body and cool you  and and thirdly if you can get hold of a  fan use a fan to keep yourself cool  go into the coolest part of the building  you're in if you happen to be in a flat  on the top floor of a building with a  flat roof above you that flat is likely  to be very hot  you need to perhaps go down into the  basement of the building which will be  the coolest place many many things need  to be done sorry to interrupt you sir  david but we don't have endless amounts  of time and i didn't want to get on to a  thought about the way that governments  are sort of dealing with this i mean you  will have seen that some of the  conservative party candidates for  leadership want to scrap the net zero  targets by 2050 i mean  balances have to be struck don't they  tough decisions have to be made but how  concerned are you that the decisions  we're making to try and save the  environment here perhaps aren't enough  and may actually get pushed back and we  won't actually meet some of the targets  we've set ourselves  i've been involved in this within  government from 2000  until 2017 so i've worked for all four  prime ministers over those that period  of time  and frankly there's a lot of nonsense  being talked about this costing our gdp  growth if we if we hit net zeros sooner  rather than later  we have found that switching across to  renewable energy sources such as  offshore wind the most successful of all  for britain offshore wind  has been a tremendous financial success  it's a wealth maker not a wealth taker  but and at the same time we're dealing  with the major problems but at the same  time  the government needs to grasp the nettle  of the importance of public services  national health service needs further  funding it's no good just exhorting  people to work harder when there's a  crisis it needs further funding we need  our health services properly funded as  the sixth wealthiest nation in the world  here we are with an ambulance service  barely operating like it should be  operating  and at the same time we need also to  understand that we have a crisis  with us now and it means that we have to  prepare to adapt to living in a hotter  climate but we also have to take  measures to see if we can somehow  re-freeze the arctic which is the group  that i've set up in in cambridge the  climate  [Music]  the center for climate repair at  cambridge is working on how we might  even re-freeze the arctic if we can do  that then stop these extreme weather  events we're buying time to allow us to  reduce emissions deeply and rapidly and  also to remove excess greenhouse gases  at scale from the atmosphere all of the  above needs the attention of government  in britain we're saying we're going to  switch over to electric vehicles  how are we going to do that if we don't  see it as a public service that there  are charging points  along streets where there's no off  street parking for the people living in  those streets we need a real focus from  government on public investment for this  public good  okay so david king do you really  appreciate your your time and expertise  this morning re-freezing the arctic is a  fascinating idea uh we don't have time  to explore it today but i'm sure we will  perhaps in our climate show at one point  uh sir david king former special  representative for climate change do  appreciate time thank you for being with  us

